\text{TF} = \frac{C_{\text{rice}}}{C_{\text{soil}}} = \frac{K_{137}^{1/2}}{f_{oc}}
\]  
where \(K_{137}\) is the retention coefficient of \(^{137}\text{CEs}\) in the soil.  
The kinetic retention coefficient \(K_{137}\) is typically \(K_{137} = 100 \times \frac{exc}{C_{exc}}\), where \(exc\) is the exchange capacity and \(C_{exc}\) is the exchange concentration of \(^{137}\)Ces.  
The exchange capacity is typically \(exc = 10 \times \frac{exchange}{organic carbon}\).  
Thus, the transfer factor can be written as:  
\[
\text{TF} = \frac{100 \times \frac{exc}{C_{exc}}} \times \frac{1}{f_{oc}} = \frac{100 \times \frac{10 \times \frac{exchange}{O_c}}{C_{exc}}} \frac{1}{f_{oc}} = \frac{1000 \times \frac{ex}{O_c}}{f_{oc} \times C_{exc}}
\]  
The typical value of \(K_{137}\) is \(100 \text{ L kg}^{-1}\).  
The average \(f_{oc}\) in soil is \(0.1\% = 0.001\).  
The average \(K_{137}\) is \(100 \text{L kg}^{-1}\).  
Thus, the average transfer factor is:  
\[
\text{TF} = \frac{100}{0.001} = 100,000
\]  
The average transfer factor is \(100,000\).  

