","When the complex `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2` dissolves in water, it dissociates into its constituent ions. The formula of the complex is `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2`. This means the central platinum (Pt) ion is bonded to four ammonia (NH3) ligands and two chloride (Cl) ligands, and there are two chloride ions outside the complex ion.

When dissolved in water, the complex ion `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]^2+` dissociates into `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]^2+` and two chloride ions `Cl^-`.

Thus, the dissociation equation is:
\[
[Pt(NH_3)_4Cl_2]Cl_2 \rightarrow [Pt(NH_3)_4Cl_2]^{2+} + 2Cl^-
\]


