","The coefficient of friction is defined as the ratio of the gravitational force (or normal force) to the gravitational force (or normal force). The normal force \( F_n \) is the force acting perpendicular to the surface of contact, and the gravitational force is \( mg \), where \( m \) is mass and \( g \) is acceleration due to gravity. The force of friction is \( f = \mu F_n \), where \( \mu \) is the coefficient of friction. Thus, the coefficient of friction is a ratio of forces.

The standard dimensional representation of force is \( [MLT^{-2}] \), where \( M \) is mass, \( L \) is length, and \( T \) is time. Since the coefficient of friction is the ratio of two forces, its units are derived from the units of force divided by the units of force. Therefore, the coefficient of friction is a dimensionless quantity.

\[
\text{Coefficient of friction} = \frac{\text{Force}}{\text{Force}} = \frac{[MLT^{-2}]}{[MLT^{-2}]} = [M^0 L^0 T^0]
\]

