</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length \(l\) of an electromagnetic field in an electromagnetic impedance boundary is given by:
\[
\frac{1}{l} = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \sqrt{\left( n_f - n_{\text{eff}} \right)^2}
\]
where \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the frequency region and \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the waveguide.

Given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu \text{m} = 1 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\)
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\)

Substituting the values:
\[
\frac{1}{l} = \frac{2\pi}{1 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}} \sqrt{(0.3)^2} = \frac{2\pi}{10^{-6}} \times 0.3
\]
\[
\frac{1}{l} = 2\pi \times 0.3 \times 10^6 = 1.885 \times 10^6 \, \text{m}^{-1}
\]
\[
l = \frac{1}{1.885 \times 10^6} = 5.30 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{m} = 530 \, \text{nm}
\]

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{530 \, \text{nm}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

