<USER_REQUEST>
A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton due to the electric field is \(F = qE\), where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\). The force is:  
\[ F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} \]  
Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we find the acceleration:  
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} \approx 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  

(b) The initial velocity is \(v_0 = 0\), the final velocity is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), and the acceleration is \(a = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\). Using the kinematic equation \(v = v_0 + at\), we solve for \(t\):  
\[ t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} \approx 7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s} \]  

Thus, the time taken is \(\boxed{7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

