","The curl of a vector vector function \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = P(x, y)\mathbf{i} + Q(x, y)\mathbf{J}\) is given by:
\[
\text{curl} \, \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \mathbf{i} + \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} \mathbf{J} + \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\mathbf{\partial y}} \mathbf{k}.
\]
where \(\mathbf{V} = V_x \mathbf{i} + V_y \mathbf{j} + V_z \mathbf{k}\).

For a vector vector function \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the \(z\) component is zero, so \(V_z = 0\). Thus, the formula simplifies to:
\[
\text{curl} \, \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \mathbf{i} + \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} \mathbf{J} + \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \mathbf{k}.
\]
Substituting \(V_z = 0\), we get:
\[
\text{curl} \, \mathbf{V} = 0 - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \mathbf{i} + \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} - 0 \mathbf{J} + \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \mathbf{k}.
\]
